g":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/IONIA%2C_Phygela._Circa_350-300_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"House of the Virgin Mary","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":37.91154,"lo":27.33406,"d":8721,"ds":"The House of the Virgin Mary (Turkish: Meryemana Evi or Meryem Ana Evi, \"Mother Mary's House\") is a Catholic shrine located on Mt. Koressos (Turkish: Bülbüldağı, \"Mount Nightingale\") in the vicinity of Ephesus, 7 kilometres (4.3 mi) from Selçuk in Turkey.","wpu":"House of the Virgin Mary","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/House_of_the_Virgin_Mary_%2813951299641%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-House_of_the_Virgin_Mary_%2813951299641%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Ephesus ( EF-uh-suhs; Ancient Greek: Έφεσος) was an ancient Greek city on the coast of Ionia, in present-day Selçuk in İzmir Province, Turkey. It was built in the 10th century BC on the site of Apasa, a city-state that was also the capital of Arzawa, by Attic and Ionian Greek colonists. During the Classical Greek era, it was one of twelve cities that were members of the Ionian League.","wpu":"Ephesus","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Ephesus_Celsus_Library_Fa%C3%A7ade.jpg/960px-Ephesus_Celsus_Library_Fa%C3%A7ade.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}